      "writtenOn": "2021-06-07T17:56:08Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Could you elaborate? IIUC, we create the new dm device using `dm`, but the new device isn\u0027t really bound to the `dm` object in the process (so destroying `dm` won\u0027t invalidate the new device).  Then, does any one need to keep track the return device/FD(?), like `File` does?  Or it could be treated as a opaque blob?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 50,
        "startChar": 15,
        "endLine": 50,
        "endChar": 48
      },
      "revId": "b5fb8d8797d941c25f9e7865de830a54c8e155bc",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "65aa831e_30632804",
        "filename": "apkverity/src/dm.rs",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 50,
      "author": {
        "id": 1132673
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-06-07T18:37:40Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The created dm block device (/dev/block/mapper/\u003cname\u003e) is not opened by the dm object. It just returns path to the block device. It\u0027s the job of the client to open it.\n\nAlso, as you pointed out, a block device created belongs to the kernel stats and therefore persists even when the dm object goes out of scope. The dm object is actually an open file descriptor to /dev/device-mapper which is just a control API.\n\nOne (even in another process) can use the control API again to get the list of all the block devices created and also to remove some of them.\n\nAmended the comment a little.",
      "parentUuid": "744cebf1_58ae7fc0",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 50,
        "startChar": 15,
        "endLine": 50,
        "endChar": 48
      },
      "revId": "b5fb8d8797d941c25f9e7865de830a54c8e155bc",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
